_acharyas","n":"11.3","t":"The Three Great Ācāryas","l":2},{"id":"sec-shankara","n":"11.4","t":"Śaṅkarācārya and Advaita Vedānta","l":2},{"id":"1141-life-and-legacy","n":"11.4.1","t":"Life and Legacy","l":3},{"id":"1142-key-principles-of-sankaras-upanisadic-interpretation","n":"11.4.2","t":"Key Principles of Śaṅkara’s Upaniṣadic Interpretation","l":3},{"id":"sec-shankara_method","n":"11.4.3","t":"Śaṅkara’s Method: Adhyāropa-Apavāda","l":3},{"id":"1144-three-levels-of-reality","n":"11.4.4","t":"Three Levels of Reality","l":3},{"id":"sec-ramanuja","n":"11.5","t":"Rāmānujācārya and Viśiṣṭādvaita Vedānta","l":2},{"id":"1151-life-and-legacy","n":"11.5.1","t":"Life and Legacy","l":3},{"id":"1152-key-principles-of-ramanujas-interpretation","n":"11.5.2","t":"Key Principles of Rāmānuja’s Interpretation","l":3},{"id":"1153-ramanujas-reading-of-tat-tvam-asi","n":"11.5.3","t":"Rāmānuja’s Reading of Tat Tvam Asi","l":3},{"id":"sec-madhva","n":"11.6","t":"Madhvācārya and Dvaita Vedānta","l":2},{"id":"1161-life-and-legacy","n":"11.6.1","t":"Life and Legacy","l":3},{"id":"1162-key-principles-of-madhvas-interpretation","n":"11.6.2","t":"Key Principles of Madhva’s Interpretation","l":3},{"id":"sec-comparative_analysis","n":"11.7","t":"Comparative Analysis","l":2},{"id":"sec-other_commentators","n":"11.8","t":"Other Important Commentators","l":2},{"id":"sec-vivarana_bhamati","n":"11.9","t":"The Vivaraṇa and Bhāmatī Sub-Schools of Advaita","l":2},{"id":"sec-western_commentary","n":"11.10","t":"Modern Western Engagement with Upaniṣadic Commentary","l":2},{"id":"sec-other_vedanta_schools","n":"11.11","t":"Other Schools of Vedānta: Nimbārka, Vallabha, and Caitanya","l":2},{"id":"11111-the-later-vedantic-acaryas","n":"11.11.1","t":"The Later Vedāntic Ācāryas","l":3},{"id":"11112-the-hermeneutical-methods-of-the-acaryas","n":"11.11.2","t":"The Hermeneutical Methods of the Ācāryas","l":3},{"id":"sec-ubhaya_vedanta","n":"11.12","t":"The Upaniṣads in Tamil: Ubhaya-Vedānta","l":2},{"id":"subsec